--- body ---
## Context

Blocked-by 0160, which fixes the instance. This is the class.

Three times in one session a prose document quoting this repo's own measurements
turned out to sit outside `bench/check_figures.py`'s `PROSE` map, so its figures
could drift with nothing able to see it:

- `DECISIONS.md` — caught by `/gaze` on PR #26. The entry drifted onto a
  superseded run (97,2 / 4 088,7 ms against the artifact's 87,6 / 4 893,9)
  within a day of being written. Worst possible home for the defect: a ratified
  entry is never edited again, so the wrong number becomes permanent.
- `RUNBOOK.md` — caught by the /roar sweep, eight values over six sites (0160).
- `CONSTRAINTS.md` — caught by review of 0160, which had recorded it as a clean
  negative. Its figures carry space thousands and no decimal comma, so the
  decimal-comma grep that cleared it could not see them.

Three independent instances of one shape is a class, and the shape is specific:
**the guard validates what someone declared, so a document nobody declared is
indistinguishable from a document with nothing to declare.** `make check` was
green throughout all three. That is the same "all-clear indistinguishable from
I-could-not-look" failure CLAUDE.md already names as this repo's most expensive
recurring defect, one level up — at the level of which documents are in scope
rather than which figures are.

## What makes this hard, and why a grep is not the answer

`FIELD-REVIEW.md` carries 24 measurement-shaped figures and belongs OUTSIDE the
guard: they are other projects' claimed numbers, surveyed, with no artifact of
ours behind them. So the rule cannot be "every .md with a decimal comma must be
in PROSE" — that fires on the survey and teaches everyone to silence it, which
is worse than no guard. The opposite rule fails too: the decimal-comma grep that
cleared `CONSTRAINTS.md` returned zero on figures written with space thousands,
and reported that as an all-clear.

The distinguishing property is provenance, not shape: a figure that MATCHES a
value in `bench/results/**` is one of ours. That is checkable — the guard already
walks those artifacts.

That provenance narrowing is also what reconciles this with `check_figures.py`'s
own docstring, which refuses to scan prose and attribute numbers because "a
check that guesses produces noise until someone turns it off". This scan does
not guess: it fires only on an exact match against a value the guard already
reads, and it reports scope, not correctness. Where the match is coincidental —
`FIELD-REVIEW.md` will collide with `bench/results/**` values by accident — a
whole-file allowlist entry may be too blunt, and the allowlist should be able to
name a file-and-figure pair with its reason.

## Actions

1. Add an adherence test that, for every tracked prose file not in `PROSE` —
   `*.md` at any depth, `tickets/**/*.erg` included — extracts
   measurement-shaped figures and fails if any matches a value present in
   `bench/results/**` at the guard's own rendering (decimal comma, space
   thousands, and the same digits without separators). Top-level `*.md` is too
   narrow to deliver the exit criterion: `verification/ACCEPTANCE-0036.md`,
   `verification/VOICE-PASS.md` and `verification/VOICE-PILOT.md` each quote
   `584 of 8 037` or `2 084,9 MiB` today, and `PROSE` already declares six
   ticket files, so tickets are established territory — these two tickets among
   them.
2. Give it an explicit allowlist with a REASON per entry, so excluding
   `FIELD-REVIEW.md` is a recorded decision rather than a silent omission.
3. Make the failure message name the file, the figure and the artifact it
   matched, so the fix is mechanical.

## Test

Red step first, and it is the whole point: run the new test against the tree as
it stood BEFORE 0160 landed and confirm it flags RUNBOOK.md. A guard written
after the fact that has never seen its own defect is not known to work. Take
that tree by reading the pre-0160 map with
`git show <pre-0160-ref>:bench/check_figures.py`, which means the test must
accept the `PROSE` map as a parameter rather than importing the module global.
Then confirm it stays silent on FIELD-REVIEW.md.

## Verification

- [ ] the test fails on the pre-0160 tree, naming RUNBOOK.md and CONSTRAINTS.md
- [ ] the test passes on the current tree
- [ ] it reaches `verification/ACCEPTANCE-0036.md` — a non-top-level file
      quoting `2 084,9 MiB` and `584 of 8 037` — so the scope cannot narrow
      back to top-level `*.md` unnoticed
- [ ] FIELD-REVIEW.md does not trigger it, and its allowlist entry states why
- [ ] a new prose doc quoting an artifact figure trips it

## Invariants

- The existing 160 declared pairs keep passing.
- No change to what `check_figures.py` does for declared documents; this adds a
  scope check above it, it does not alter the anchor logic.

## Exit criteria

- A prose document that quotes one of this repo's measurements cannot sit
  outside the figure guard without an allowlist entry giving the reason.
